WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:03.349
Hello and welcome to our webcast,

00:00:03.349 --> 00:00:07.124
MBA Student Insights:
International Students at the GSB.

00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:11.415
My name is Tong Zhang, and I'm a first
year MBA student here at the GSB.

00:00:11.415 --> 00:00:15.354
I'm joined by my classmate Artem and
Michael, also known by Hobbsy,

00:00:15.354 --> 00:00:19.026
will be talking about the student
experience here at the GSB, and

00:00:19.026 --> 00:00:22.400
we'll answer as many of
your questions as we can.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.880
So send in a question to us about
what it's like to be a student.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.050
Just click the chat button at the top
of your screen, enter the question, and

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.810
click send to all panelists.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.270
Please be aware that we won't be able
to talk about admission process,

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.350
applications, financial aid,
or the visa process today.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.450
You can consult the website or
contact the MBA Admissions office for

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.320
that information.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.710
So let's begin with introductions.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.070
Where we are from, what we were each
doing before coming to the GSB, and

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.905
the what are our goals after graduation.

00:00:56.905 --> 00:00:58.360
I'll start.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.550
So I grew up in China.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.920
I came to the states 6 years
ago to study journalism.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.660
Before the GSB,
I was a tech NMA reporter in New York.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.408
After the GSB I plan to move into tech and

00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:15.675
help build tech enabled platforms to
help distribution of media content.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:16.879
How about you?

00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.570
&gt;&gt; Perfect, thank you Tong for
the introduction.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.770
So my name is Artem I'm
from Russia originally.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:25.555
Most of my life I lived in Moscow.

00:01:25.555 --> 00:01:28.890
And the career wise before GSB.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.140
I was mainly just being company, so
I spent more than four years management

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.180
consulting across Europe and
also South Africa office.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.984
And regarding the post GSB goals, I am
still considering my different options.

00:01:41.984 --> 00:01:42.530
&gt;&gt; Hobbsy?

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so thank you very much for
your introduction.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.440
I am from New Zealand originally
I was a professional rugby player

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.810
before coming to GSB.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.910
Five of those years in New Zealand and
then the last three in Japan.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:03.330
With regard to my post graduation goals,
I am currently exploring a career

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:08.040
in the real estate industry, which I'll
be doing an internship over the summer.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.983
And hopefully sort of
proceeding from there.

00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:12.365
&gt;&gt; Nice.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:16.360
All right, so we'll start with some
questions submitted by the audience.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.910
So the first question will be,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.990
did you guys consider schools out
of the US when you were applying?

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.930
What was your consideration between
US schools and non US schools, and

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.460
why did you choose Stanford?

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.555
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm happy to start from this one.

00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:37.520
So, as they said, right,
most of my life I spent in Europe, and

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.470
obviously I considered
European schools as well.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.570
The way my decision process went, so

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.740
basically since I spent all of the time
in Europe, just the idea of spending some

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.170
time in America was more exciting to me
because it was new geographically for me.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.945
And also, when I saw thought about the
value of MB, I saw what kind of doors, and

00:02:55.945 --> 00:02:58.813
means of opportunity that
can be opened for me.

00:02:58.813 --> 00:03:03.067
And I had a perception that and American
MBA, it opens some doors in America,

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:06.537
but also in Europe and Asia,
it's kind of like global degree,

00:03:06.537 --> 00:03:11.500
whereas European and Asian schools, they
are more kind of like one region focused.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.321
So from that kind of
flexibility perspective,

00:03:15.321 --> 00:03:17.811
American MBA made sense to me.

00:03:17.811 --> 00:03:18.798
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:22.597
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no I also explored sort
of MBA programs outside the US,

00:03:22.597 --> 00:03:25.680
mainly in the UK focused on Cambridge and
Oxford.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.360
I think one of the biggest
things Ivan touched on

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.810
a few of them are the global reach.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.210
It was a big one for me.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:38.364
But also the fact I really like the two
year program versus the one year, so

00:03:38.364 --> 00:03:39.780
the 12 month program.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.795
Reason for that coming from
the non-traditional background,

00:03:43.795 --> 00:03:47.586
I like the idea of being able to, it's
been my first year exploring industries,

00:03:47.586 --> 00:03:51.360
having some internship to explore that to
see if that is what I really want to do.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.720
And having a second year if I could
change that to something else.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.480
For further, I continue down that track.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.268
&gt;&gt; That's okay.
So how about why Stanford?

00:04:01.268 --> 00:04:01.800
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.720
&gt;&gt; I couldn't talk about my experience.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.155
So I've been working in
the US before GSB so

00:04:06.155 --> 00:04:10.400
my choice was supposed mostly
focusing on US schools.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.097
And I know I want to move into tag and
I feel Standford the proximity to

00:04:15.097 --> 00:04:19.170
Silicon Valley will be a great place,
and also I'm really

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:24.260
drawn to the spirit of personal reflection
and committee building here at GSB.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:27.663
So that's what's one of my inspirations.

00:04:27.663 --> 00:04:30.031
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so for me,

00:04:30.031 --> 00:04:31.817
it's quite an easy question, and

00:04:31.817 --> 00:04:35.920
I think the answer going to be very
similar to what Tong just said, so.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.170
First of all,
it's definitely about Silicon Valley,

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.170
to a large extent,
like a very exciting place to live in,

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.760
considering how many cool
things are happening around.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.460
And also, I was very fortunate to meet
many people from the GSB community,

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.720
even before applying to the school.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.818
And I just,
I felt I clicked with those people and

00:04:54.818 --> 00:04:57.407
I felt I shared all the values with them.

00:04:57.407 --> 00:04:59.438
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; Which is the idea of spending of two

00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:03.044
years in the community of disabled
people was very attractive to me.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:09.920
Many of the same points feel that
adds will be, one, the class size.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.700
I really like the fact that it was
a size of 400 a class, that's 800,

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.060
900 in some of the top schools in
the US I thought that intimacy,

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.720
getting to know classmates,
was a big thing for me.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.310
Second, location, in California.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.680
I mean, and the campus,
the amazing campus we have.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.720
You take one walk around here,
it's pretty hard to sort of turn away and

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.620
say you'd like to go somewhere else.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.170
And then thirdly,

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:36.350
it was just the caliber of students
that I was going to be alongside.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.340
So for me,
it was from a non traditional background,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.730
wanting to explore different industries.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.096
So that I would sort of,
the tidal waves or

00:05:43.096 --> 00:05:45.880
boats being around people
really high caliber.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.585
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:05:48.585 --> 00:05:51.450
Yeah, I see with the other question.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:56.560
So, how has been the transition
to the ESL of education been?

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.000
Is it hard for you,
adjusting from what you were used to?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:01.500
&gt;&gt; Mm hm.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:07.780
I can start here, so
I actually studied abroad in the US, for

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.890
one semester back in 2011.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:14.580
So I'd had some exposure to it previously,
but

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.910
honestly for me,
it wasn't that big of a transition.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.844
I've done higher education in New Zealand
and Australia previously as well, and

00:06:21.844 --> 00:06:23.213
it was very similar teaching styles.

00:06:23.213 --> 00:06:26.240
Very similar curriculum.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.110
So it wasn't a huge transition for me.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:33.050
&gt;&gt; I would say for
me it was like difficult as well.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:37.530
So before, there was an academic
experience, I started Elise.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.910
Being multi-defined over there.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.035
And I would say that over all
the experience is quite similar.

00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:44.915
The only thing that surprised
me quite a lot is that,

00:06:44.915 --> 00:06:47.785
I would say like the intensity
of the education.

00:06:47.785 --> 00:06:52.000
At GSB it stands that if I'd ever sit,
for example,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.120
we have one course that runs for all the
whole year and then we have an exam then.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.870
Here we have three quarters.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.200
Here a long academic year and within
each quarter you have the final exam.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.750
You have the mid term and
you have a lot of group assignments.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.940
So like the intensity of
the overall process is

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.530
something that surprised me quite a lot,
but

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:13.530
I assume that's pretty much from where the
experience and the knowledge comes from.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.444
&gt;&gt; Yeah I would just add one point,
I feel the class participation or

00:07:17.444 --> 00:07:21.980
case based discussion is find something
I feel struggled a bit to adjust.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:25.600
Because coming from Asian culture from
China you were supposed to sit there and

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.410
just find some questions when
your teacher asks you to.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:33.909
But that way I feel I need to push myself
a little bit to like raise my hand and

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:35.210
jump in.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:35.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:37.770
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:42.020
So how about talk about how
welcoming do you feel about the GSB

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.279
community to internationals?

00:07:44.279 --> 00:07:45.670
&gt;&gt; I'm happy to start on this one.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:54.850
I think, first of all, like 40% of people
at GSB are from international background,

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:58.830
which makes GSB by definition kind of
welcoming to the international community,

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.420
because it's an international community.

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.650
I also would say that GSB,
has quite a lot of tools

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:10.775
to involve people and to make them
part of the broader community.

00:08:10.775 --> 00:08:12.346
By tools, what do I mean by tools.

00:08:12.346 --> 00:08:15.506
First of all sections,
the fact that we start for

00:08:15.506 --> 00:08:18.821
the first quarter and
we are starting the section,

00:08:18.821 --> 00:08:24.081
70 people who we got to know quite well
over the first two months of the program.

00:08:24.081 --> 00:08:27.585
And then you have a squad which
becomes your inner family,

00:08:27.585 --> 00:08:30.529
seven people who you know really,
really well.

00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:32.457
Also, you have cooperativity.

00:08:32.457 --> 00:08:35.570
Most of the people live on campus so
you bond with many people and

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:39.003
build smaller communities within
the residential area you live in.

00:08:39.003 --> 00:08:42.218
So there are quite a few tools that
incorporate you into the broader

00:08:42.218 --> 00:08:42.857
community.

00:08:42.857 --> 00:08:46.444
&gt;&gt; Yeah, echoing a lot of
those points again, I think,

00:08:46.444 --> 00:08:51.251
I live on campus directly across
the street from where we take classes.

00:08:51.251 --> 00:08:56.300
I think I'm sort of one of a couple
of international students in my wing.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.850
So there's that forced
interaction with US students,

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:04.438
with other international students which
makes it such a welcoming community.

00:09:04.438 --> 00:09:06.980
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And I'd also say the support staff we

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.500
have in the MBA Office have been amazing
helping us if we wanted to talk to

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.550
an immigration lawyer,
putting on different sort of BBLs and

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.640
different events that if we have any
questions that need to be answered.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.680
And then again,
in the greater Stanford community,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.990
having been to international
center's been a great resource.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:25.240
So yeah, felt very well with that.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:26.520
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's great.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.840
Yeah, you guys both mentioned
about the social aspect.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.100
But how about the classroom setting?

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.198
How do you think the academic experience
has been incorporated into international

00:09:35.198 --> 00:09:36.847
experience into or integrated?

00:09:36.847 --> 00:09:38.089
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:38.089 --> 00:09:42.705
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] Yeah, so I mean, I'm in a
class right now, strategy beyond markets.

00:09:42.705 --> 00:09:45.640
It's essentially focused on
everything international.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:50.085
We have one or two classes on the US
healthcare system but besides that,

00:09:50.085 --> 00:09:52.616
it's all around nternational issues.

00:09:52.616 --> 00:10:00.477
We did a case last week on Splash
mobile networks with payment system.

00:10:00.477 --> 00:10:01.268
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:01.268 --> 00:10:02.173
&gt;&gt; In Ghana.

00:10:02.173 --> 00:10:05.389
I feel that it has been
incorporated in different classes.

00:10:05.389 --> 00:10:09.699
They're always trying to incorporate
at least a few international cases into

00:10:09.699 --> 00:10:10.620
the classwork.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:12.707
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And just a couple of words from my

00:10:12.707 --> 00:10:14.320
experience.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:19.120
My experience, most of the classes I'm
taking, yeah I would say they are probably

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.900
more tilted towards
the American companies' cases.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:26.695
But at the same time I think most
of the cases, they teach there,

00:10:26.695 --> 00:10:31.419
they teach quite universal lessons which
are applicable to the global issues.

00:10:31.419 --> 00:10:35.066
So I wouldn't say that I personally
consider that to be an issue.

00:10:35.066 --> 00:10:38.351
&gt;&gt; Yeah, because the knowledge
of the skills, the framework.

00:10:38.351 --> 00:10:39.211
&gt;&gt; Quite universal, yeah.

00:10:39.211 --> 00:10:40.758
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:10:40.758 --> 00:10:42.884
because we're talking about the academics,

00:10:42.884 --> 00:10:46.920
maybe we can also touch up on your
favorite academic experience so far.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.000
I can start off.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.048
So I really love the group projects
a lot of the classes ask us to do.

00:10:53.048 --> 00:10:57.210
So last quarter, I took the data and
decisions pilot class.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:03.160
So we are supposed to help alumni's
company and to do a regression project.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.310
So in the whole quarter,

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.850
I worked with four other
classmates to do a whole R-model.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.950
For me,
coming from a journalism background,

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:12.730
that was a huge learning experience.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.040
I enjoy working with people,

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:17.890
working with my classmates and see
a project actually helping a company grow.

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:19.070
So that's my favorite experience.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:23.060
How about Artem, do you have any?

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:27.090
&gt;&gt; I think I enjoyed most of the classes
that are heavy on the number

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.210
of guest speakers.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:34.220
In particular the two classes on the top
of my mind would be the product launch,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.930
the advanced marketing class.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.013
And also the money making class.

00:11:38.013 --> 00:11:43.127
So the product launch one, we had quite
a few entrepreneurial guest speakers,

00:11:43.127 --> 00:11:45.658
which every conversation is.

00:11:45.658 --> 00:11:47.778
And also the money and banking class,

00:11:47.778 --> 00:11:51.730
there are quite a few guest
speakers from the Federal Bank.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:56.360
So I think that there's elements who
have access to the practitioners

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.395
from the industry, this is something
that I benefited most from.

00:11:59.395 --> 00:11:59.949
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:11:59.949 --> 00:12:03.271
&gt;&gt; Yeah I mean there's a couple
classes that come to mind for me.

00:12:03.271 --> 00:12:06.789
I really enjoyed organization
behavior in the first quarter.

00:12:06.789 --> 00:12:10.875
Franklin, and I think is one of
the greatest officials going around and

00:12:10.875 --> 00:12:13.667
I loved how just applicable
all that material was

00:12:13.667 --> 00:12:15.793
to daily interactions with people.

00:12:15.793 --> 00:12:20.371
I really enjoyed sports,
business management class last quarter.

00:12:20.371 --> 00:12:23.091
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Again, guest speakers amazing,

00:12:23.091 --> 00:12:27.690
GM of the Warriors, owner of
the San Francisco Giants, just amazing,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.388
amazing guest speakers.

00:12:29.388 --> 00:12:34.250
And I'm thinking that's one of the great
things about Stanford is that we have that

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.621
ability to bring in that kind of talent.

00:12:36.621 --> 00:12:39.809
And then the class that I'm in right now,
real estate investment class.

00:12:39.809 --> 00:12:44.698
We've been given 24A just north of
Milpitas, just 30 minutes south from here.

00:12:44.698 --> 00:12:49.568
Having to put together a development plan,
being given an alumni mentor and

00:12:49.568 --> 00:12:54.057
working through that process,
these real world applicable skills,

00:12:54.057 --> 00:12:56.584
which I think has been really amazing.

00:12:56.584 --> 00:13:01.291
&gt;&gt; Great, let's change gear a bit and
talk about student activities, so

00:13:01.291 --> 00:13:05.461
talk about your favorite
extracurricular activities so far.

00:13:05.461 --> 00:13:09.272
I can start off because last Friday
we just had the GSB show and

00:13:09.272 --> 00:13:11.459
I'm part of the leadership team.

00:13:11.459 --> 00:13:12.853
I was the stage manager.

00:13:12.853 --> 00:13:17.440
Though a little bit background at the GSB
show is the student run original musical.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:20.930
So we put together the whole script and
the whole production.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:21.801
It was in the Fox Theater.

00:13:21.801 --> 00:13:23.925
We had 1200 people coming.

00:13:23.925 --> 00:13:25.376
It was a great production.

00:13:25.376 --> 00:13:29.233
I feel super excited to be part of that
and just feel like be in that experience

